A RESOLUTION OF TATTNALL COUNTY BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS TO AUTHORIZE AND ENACT MORATORIUM RELATED TO LAND USE AND DEVELOPMENT INVOLVING NON-TRADITIONAL HOUSING

_ STRUCTURES; TO PROVIDE AN EFFECTIVE DATE; AND FOR OTHER PURPOSES ALLOWED BY LAW.

WHEREAS, as provided in the Georgia Constitution, the Tattnall County Board of Commissioners (“BOC”) enjoys home rule powers, including police power, and land development and governmental and supplementary powers, and the power to adopt such ordinances and regulations as necessary to perform all functions of

- government in order to promote and protect the health, safety and general welfare of the citizens and general public of Tattnall County (“County”);

WHEREAS, the BOC finds that the ordered regulation of residential, commercial and/or industrial development is necessary to protect the public health, safety, welfare and property values of the citizens of the County;

' WHEREAS, the BOC from time to time reviews its ordinances to ensure that its laws preserve the quality of life for citizens while imposing limited and effective regulations on certain residential, commercial and/or industrial development and uses that impact quality of life;

WHEREAS, the rapid land development in counties and cities near or adjoining

_ the County has led to widespread population growth, which, in turn, has led to an increased need to provide housing for temporary or transient workers, and for individuals who seek to purchase, lease or otherwise occupy housing in the County’s unincorporated areas on a longer-term basis;

WHEREAS, longstanding inflation in the United States has caused increasing numbers of Americans to move to rural Georgia counties, like the County, where

~ they can to obtain land at cheaper prices and then use and develop real property for non-traditional housing, including tent communities, yurts, Quonset huts, containers homes, park model homes, tree houses, glamping facilities and tiny houses (hereinafter all of the aforesaid structures are collectively called “nontraditional housing”);

-WHEREAS, in light of the growing need for housing, the Governing Authority has recently been confronted with genuine interest or desire on the part of one or more individuals to receive approval to use or develop land in the County’s unincorporated areas for non-traditional housing, including housing or a structure known asa “tiny house” (a tiny house is typically defined asa single-family home, in general 400 square feet or less, excluding lofts);

WHEREAS, the BOC intends to study the effects of non-traditional housing on the public health, safety and welfare of the County's residents and businesses,

