WHEREAS, the BOC intends to consider revisions to its Code of Ordinances (“Code”) designed to promote and protect the public health, safety and welfare of the County's residents and businesses, including concerns regarding persons or entities establishing, constructing, installing, placing, developing and occupying

_ non-traditional housing and the impacts of same on the County's residents and businesses, through zoning classifications and/or regulations and standards for distance, density, environmental impacts’ and other ancillary impacts of nontraditional housing on surrounding properties;

WHEREAS, the BOC finds that it must take immediate and responsive action to protect the County, and it is in the best interests of the health, safety and welfare . ‘of the citizens of the County thatno applications be accepted in the unincorporated areas of the County for a period of 180 days from the effective date of this resolution if or where such applications relate to the establishing, constructing, installing, placing, developing and/or occupying non-traditional housing or such non-traditional housing exists or is under construction, installation, placement and development, including any applications for special land use permits, rezonings, zonings, land disturbance permits, building permits, business licenses, or - certificates of occupancy;

WHEREAS, the BOC finds that it is in the County’s best interests and that the aims and goals provided herein are best served by declaring and adopting a temporary and limited moratorium to protect the public as a temporary measure to preserve the status quo;

WHEREAS, the BOC desires to strike an appropriate balance between the development of real property for non-traditional housing and any public concerns related thereto;

WHEREAS, in City of Roswell et al. v. Outdoor Systems, Inc., 274 Ga. 130 (2001), the Georgia Supreme Court held that limited moratoria are reasonable and do not

' constitute land use when such moratoria are’ applied throughout the County; as well, the notice requirements of the Georgia Zoning Procedures Law do not apply to such moratoria;
